✅ What is Redis?
Redis (Remote Dictionary Server) is an in-memory data store used as:

A cache

A database

A message broker

It’s known for being extremely fast because it stores all data in RAM, not on disk.

🔥 Why Use Redis?
💨 Super fast (microseconds latency)

🧠 Stores data in memory

📦 Supports complex data types: strings, lists, sets, hashes, sorted sets

🔁 Persistence available (optional)

👥 Supports Pub/Sub and Streams (messaging)

🧰 Built-in eviction policies (great for caching)

📌 Common Use Cases
| Use Case           | Description                                |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------ |
| Caching            | Cache DB results, session data, etc.       |
| Leaderboards       | Sorted sets for top rankings               |
| Rate Limiting      | Track API request counts                   |
| Distributed Locks  | To avoid race conditions                   |
| Message Queues     | Using Redis Pub/Sub or Streams             |
| Session Management | Especially in Spring Boot, Express.js apps |


🔧 Redis Data Types
| Type       | Example Use                     |
| ---------- | ------------------------------- |
| String     | Cache page HTML or tokens       |
| List       | Message queues                  |
| Set        | Unique items like tags          |
| Sorted Set | Leaderboards with scores        |
| Hash       | User objects or key-value pairs |


✅ Redis with Spring Boot
1. Dependency (in pom.xml)

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-redis</artifactId>
</dependency>

2. application.yml

spring:
  redis:
    host: localhost
    port: 6379

3. Service Example

@Service
public class RedisService {

    @Autowired
    private StringRedisTemplate redisTemplate;

    public void save(String key, String value) {
        redisTemplate.opsForValue().set(key, value);
    }

    public String get(String key) {
        return redisTemplate.opsForValue().get(key);
    }
}

🧠 Advanced Use: Caching with Annotations
Add cache dependency:

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-cache</artifactId>
</dependency>


Enable caching:

@SpringBootApplication
@EnableCaching
public class App {}

Use caching in service:

@Cacheable(value = "users", key = "#id")
public User getUserById(Long id) {
    // only runs if not in cache
    return userRepository.findById(id).orElse(null);
}

